  "content": {
    "id": "p48-q9-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Find all values of $k$ for which $(k + 2)x^2 + x - 3 = 0$ has 2 real solutions.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k > -\\dfrac{25}{12}$, with $k \\ne -2$. Two distinct real solutions require $\\Delta>0$ and a nonzero quadratic coefficient.",
      "worked": "For a quadratic equation, $k+2\\ne0$, so $k\\ne-2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\Delta &= 1^2-4(k+2)(-3)\\\\\n&=12k+25.\n\\end{align*}$$\nTwo distinct real solutions require $\\Delta>0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n12k+25 &>0\\\\\n12k &>-25\\\\\nk &>-\\frac{25}{12}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $k=-2$, the equation becomes $x-3=0$, which has only one solution. Therefore $k>-\\dfrac{25}{12}$, with $k\\ne-2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
